WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.140
Google just gave its ai the power to click, type, and organize files on your Mac while you watch. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 233. How the clicking gets learned, two free experiments, and quick bits follow. Google The Verge Reports added a feature called Computer Use to its Gemini app on Mac computers. Gemini is Google's artificial intelligence or ai assistant. Computer Use lets it steer the cursor, launch programs, change documents, and surf the web for you.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.760
Picture a helpful friend at your keyboard who follows instructions one step at a time.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.120
You stay in control and you can halt it or give a yes before a bigger step.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.040
It runs in the background so your own screen and pointer remain yours.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.100
It has its own background window with a live preview in your chat, and you choose which

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:47.940
apps it may touch.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.080
You no longer have to copy information from one app to another by hand.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.600
You can ask it to sort files into folders, fill out forms, or repeat the same chore across

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.860
a batch of files. It can build a slideshow from material in different places, or list items

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.920
for sale. For school, that can mean turning notes into a presentation without hopping between

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.300
tabs. The feature is rolling out now in English in the United States. You need the Gemini app

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.880
and a paid Google AI Plus Pro or Ultra Plan. Open AI and Thropic and Meta already let their

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:26.160
AIS work inside desktop apps, so Google is arriving a little late. Download the Gemini app for Mac

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.720
from Google, open a chat, and ask it to organize my desktop files by date.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.980
Watch the preview window, and you can stop at any time.

00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:38.899
Training on millions of computer sessions is what lets the AI pick each click.

00:01:39.379 --> 00:01:43.679
In a video game, your character walks around and taps objects while you just press buttons.

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:46.279
You start by telling it the job you want done.

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:49.699
Then it studies what is on the screen, the way your eyes would.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:54.739
After that, it matches that view to habits it learned, like opening a folder and dragging

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:55.679
files into it.

00:01:55.679 --> 00:01:59.159
Last, it chooses the next click or the next keys to type.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:03.919
Your button presses move a game character, and here your instruction plus those past

00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:05.339
examples move the cursor.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:10.359
Huge pattern matching, pointed at ordinary screen chores, is really all that is going

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:10.639
on.

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:17.285
Your hands can stay busy because chat GPT, the chatbot from OpenAI, now types while you

00:02:17.285 --> 00:02:17.525
talk.

00:02:18.165 --> 00:02:23.405
OpenAI added live dictation, which turns your spoken words into text as you go, instead

00:02:23.405 --> 00:02:25.005
of waiting until you finish recording.

00:02:25.005 --> 00:02:29.085
That helps when you think faster than you type, or when you like voice notes.

00:02:29.705 --> 00:02:35.465
It is rolling out now globally on every chat GPT plan, on the web, the desktop app, and

00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:36.985
